  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1... \nA cold front, currently moving across central Michigan, is \nexpected to reach our region late this evening. As the front \napproaches, a squall line is expected to develop near Lake Erie \nand track south. Damaging straight-line winds will be the \nprimary hazard. Secondary threats include quarter sized hail, \nbrief embedded tornadoes along the cold front, and locally heavy\nrainfall. \n\nThe Storm Prediction Center has placed areas north of Interstate\n70 in an Enhanced Risk (3/5) for severe weather, where the \norientation of the wind shear relative to the front is most \nfavorable for producing widespread damaging straight-line winds.\nNumerous communities could experience downed trees and \nscattered power outages this evening as the squall line moves \nthrough. \n\nAhead of the approaching cold front, a few discrete\nthunderstorms may develop. If these storms produce updrafts that\nrapidly strengthen they could generate strong, ice-loaded \ndowndrafts capable of producing severe wind gusts.\n\nThe higher elevations of western PA and northern WV, and their\nadjacent lowlands, are still forecast to receive the heaviest \nrainfall accumulations of the day per latest convective allowing\nmodels, primarily due to a combination of afternoon rounds of\nthunderstorms and training along the slowly-advancing evening \ncold front. The Weather Prediction Center has upgraded their \nexcessive rainfall outlook to a Slight Risk (Level 2 out of 4) \nfor these areas, and a Flood Watch has been issued until \nMidnight tonight.\n\nThe probability of thunderstorms is expected to decrease between\n9pm and 11pm this evening as the cold front exits south into\ncentral West Virginia.\n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...\nMachine learning guidance has remained consistent in signaling \nthat a low pressure system tracking across the Great Lakes on \nTuesday will bring another threat of severe storms to the \nregion. The Storm Prediction Center (SPC) has outlined the \nentire forecast domain in a 15% severe weather risk. Damaging \nwind gusts and tornadoes appear to the be the primary hazard as \nstrong wind shear accompanies this large-scale system. \n\nIn addition to the severe weather threat, there is also the\npotential for training showers and storms with a potential\nmesoscale convection system (MCS) across the Ohio River Valley.\nRainfall totals could range between 1.5-2 inches in a short \nperiod of time and result in flash flooding.",
